**Multiple sclerosis (MS)** is a condition where the **immune system attacks the protective layer (myelin)** around the nerves in the brain and spinal cord. This disrupts messages between the brain and the body. More than **150,000 people** in the UK have MS. It is often diagnosed in people in their 20s, 30s and 40s, and is more common in women.

## Symptoms

Symptoms vary a lot from person to person:

- **fatigue**, which is very common
- problems with **sight**, such as blurred vision or pain moving the eye (optic neuritis)
- **numbness or tingling**
- **muscle weakness**, stiffness or spasms
- problems with **balance** and walking
- **bladder and bowel** problems
- problems with **thinking**, memory and concentration
- pain, and changes in mood

## Types of MS

- **relapsing remitting MS**, the most common type, where symptoms flare up (**relapses**) and then improve
- **secondary progressive MS**, where symptoms slowly get worse over time after a period of relapses
- **primary progressive MS**, where symptoms get worse from the start

## Getting diagnosed

A **neurologist** will examine you and arrange an **MRI scan**. You may also have a **lumbar puncture** or tests of how your nerves respond. It can take time to get a diagnosis.

Read about [MS treatment](/conditions/brain-nerves-and-senses/multiple-sclerosis/treatment/).
